A hypervisor (also known as a Virtual Machine Monitor or VMM) is software or firmware that creates and runs Virtual Machines (VMs).It allows one physical computer to safely share its hardware (CPU, RAM, storage, network) so you can run multiple separate operating systems (like Windows, Linux, and macOS) at the same time on the exact same machine.The Real-Estate AnalogyWithout a hypervisor (Standard PC): A single-family house. One family (one Operating System) uses the entire property.With a hypervisor: An apartment building. The building manager (the Hypervisor) divides the physical space into isolated apartments (Virtual Machines). Each tenant lives independently without knowing what the others are doing inside their units.The Two Types of Hypervisors1. Type 1: Bare-Metal HypervisorsWhere it runs: Directly on the physical server hardware—there is no standard OS underneath it.Performance: Extremely fast, secure, and resource-efficient.Where it's used: Enterprise data centers, cloud providers (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud).Examples: VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM, Xen / Proxmox.2. Type 2: Hosted HypervisorsWhere it runs: As an app installed on top of your existing operating system (like Windows or macOS).Performance: Slower, because it has to share resources through the host OS.Where it's used: Personal laptops, testing environments, student labs, and malware analysis sandboxes.Examples: Oracle VirtualBox, VMware Workstation / Fusion, UTM, Parallels.
